.TH veldxf "" "Command"
.SH NAME
veldxf \- convert between DXF and vellum drawings
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBveldxf\fR [\fB\-layer\fR \fIname=n\fR] [\fB\-scale\fR \fIN\fR] \fIfile.dxf\fR > \fIfile.d\fR
.br
\fBveldxf \-x\fR \fIfile.d ...\fR > \fIfile.dxf\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
veldxf -- convert between DXF and vellum drawings
Usage:	veldxf [ -layer name=n ] [ -scale N ] file.dxf > file.d
	veldxf -x file.d ... > file.dxf
Options:
	-layer name=n	fold DXF layer `name' onto vellum layer n (0-3)
	-scale N	drawing units per grid unit (default 1 = 1 mm)
	-x	the other direction: drawings OUT as DXF
.HE
.B veldxf
is the border with the office CAD, and it crosses in both directions.
Read a DXF file (the R10-era plain-text format) and it prints an
ordinary
.B vellum
drawing on standard output; give it
.B \-x
and it reads drawings and prints a DXF ENTITIES section instead.
The two directions are one program because they are one format, and
because the pair has to FIX-POINT: a drawing exported and re-imported
is the same drawing, and the shipped regression gate proves it on
every run.
.PP
Conversion is not a link.
Run it once; the result is a drawing like any other, and nothing
remembers where it came from.
.SH IMPORT
The scope is the honest entity subset:
.DS
.ta 14n
	LINE	a vellum L line
	CIRCLE	a C circle
	ARC	an A arc (centre, radius and angles land exactly)
	TEXT	a T label at the nearest of the three sizes
	POLYLINE	a P polyline (LWPOLYLINE too; split at the
		20-point ceiling)
.DE
Everything else is SKIPPED AND COUNTED: the run ends with a report
like ``dropped: 12 SPLINE, 3 INSERT'' on the diagnostic output,
because a silent partial import is how drawings lose limbs.
Flatten blocks at the source; a SPLINE can be chorded by whatever
exported it.
.PP
DXF layer names fold onto vellum's four layers through the
.B \-layer
map; a bare digit name (``0''..``3'') folds onto itself and anything
unmapped lands on layer 0.
.B \-scale
gives the number of drawing units in one grid unit \- the default 1
takes one unit as one millimetre \- and the matching
.B U
units header is emitted.
Coordinates may carry decimals; they are handled in fixed point (no
floating point anywhere).
The drawing's minimum corner lands at grid 0,0 with the y axis
flipped (DXF y runs up).
.SH EXPORT
.B \-x
writes the sheets named after it into ONE ENTITIES section on
standard output, the way a set prints onto one roll.
Coordinates are in GRID units at a pinned quarter-unit resolution, so
symbol geometry emits exactly as .25 steps; y is flipped (DXF y runs
up); arcs are true ARC entities, not chords, because a chorded arc is
correct on paper and wrong in a file another CAD will edit; and our
layer number is the DXF layer name.
Layers marked no-print, and the construction layer, are skipped.
.SH EXAMPLES
.DS
.ta 8n
	veldxf plate.dxf > plate.d
	veldxf -x bracket.d > bracket.dxf
	veldxf -x amp1.d amp2.d > amps.dxf
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B vellum ,
.B velplot ,
.B velpic ,
.B zfile
